    "//2": "EVERY field effect below MUST carry both `body_part` and `is_environmental` explicitly -- do not 'tidy' them away as redundant defaults, because BN does not apply the defaults you would expect.  field_type.cpp:154-155 reads both with the FOUR-argument optional(), whose contract is `if( !was_loaded ) { member = MemberType(); }` (generic_factory.h:786-795): when the key is absent it DEFAULT-CONSTRUCTS, discarding the field_effect struct's own initialisers (field_type.h:87-88).  So an omitted `body_part` becomes bodypart_str_id() == \"\" -- NOT NULL_ID(), which is the valid \"num_bp\" (string_id_null_ids.cpp:64) -- and an omitted `is_environmental` becomes bool() == false, not the declared true.  An empty bodypart then blows up downstream on any effect outside hardcoded_effects' map, because player_hardcoded_effects.cpp:527 does an unguarded `it.get_bp()->token`, and again in Creature::store (savegame_json.cpp:3730) which serialises the effect keyed on it, so the bad bodypart PERSISTS INTO THE SAVE.  Reported from a player's debug.log 2026-07-29 as 'invalid body part id \"\"' repeating every turn.  Vanilla BN never trips this because all 18 of its field effects specify body_part; upstream MoM omits it and DDA is quiet about it.  The first entry of each intensity level here was the one missing body_part -- torso, the gap in the head/arm_l/arm_r/leg_l/leg_r set.  is_environmental is pinned to false because that is what BN was ALREADY doing by accident; pinning it keeps behaviour identical and stops it flipping if BN ever fixes optional().  Note that upstream's intent was probably environmental (six entries only makes sense as six rolls against six bodyparts' fire resistance), so flipping these to true is a live balance question -- but it IS a balance change, not a bug fix.",
